# EOL pins for files whose on-disk bytes are pinned by SHA or rebuilt with LF
# in tests. Without this, Windows clones with core.autocrlf=true check these
# out as CRLF and break byte-exact comparisons.
#
# HISTORICAL NOTE (2026-07-31): the pin below was originally justified by the
# "F01 round-trip / hash-sentinel tests in tests/test_ingest.py". That suite was
# DELETED 2026-06-25 with the Notion ingest subsystem (c6af48f), and the two
# tracked fixtures it covers (counterfactuals_fixture.jsonl,
# signals_fixture_fixed_ids.jsonl) now have no loader anywhere in the tree. The
# pin is retained as inert-but-harmless rather than removed with its fixtures;
# retire all three together if they are ever pruned. Scope kept narrow so
# vendor-data CRLF hashing under
# data/tv_exports/, data/bar_data/, data/external/ (see CLAUDE.md "Vendor-data
# integrity gate") is unaffected.
tests/fixtures/*.jsonl text eol=lf

# Secondary-calendar wrapper binds this tracked JSON by its raw SHA-256.
ops/calendars/cme_holiday_calendar_2022_2026.json text eol=lf

# M1 monitoring acceptance pin (docs/notes/rail_build/M1_MONITORING_ACCEPTANCE.json
# fixture_hashes): all 6 pinned files hash content that is byte-identical to the
# current git blob once CRLF is normalized to LF (verified 2026-08-20) — the last
# pin refresh ran on a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true, so every pinned
# hash is the right code, wrong line ending. Linux CI (and the Fly host's Linux
# container) checks these out as LF, so a Windows-refreshed pin will always read
# as skew there even with zero real content drift. eol=lf pins future refreshes
# to LF regardless of the refreshing machine's autocrlf setting, closing the class
# of bug going forward. Does NOT retroactively fix the fixture_hashes already
# recorded in the JSON pin — per that artifact's own documented discipline, a pin
# is only rewritten in the same motion as a real redeploy, from hashes read
# IN-CONTAINER, never from local/tree bytes.
tests/ops/test_m1_acceptance_drills.py text eol=lf
ops/c1_rail/*.py text eol=lf

# Hash-gated analysis CSVs: WAVE1_SHA256SUMS is LF-blob-based, so autocrlf checkout
# (CRLF) fails the harness hash-gate in a fresh worktree — and would halt a Cursor
# dispatch. eol=lf pins the checkout to LF (matches the blob), is renormalize-safe,
# and stays diffable. Scope kept narrow (this dir only); broaden per hash-gated dir
# after confirming its manifest is LF-based. Vendor data (data/*) stays CRLF-hashed.
lab/analysis/aegis_6j_prop_reconstruction_2026-07/*.csv text eol=lf

# GROW-0 frozen grammar (discovery_manifests/grow0_grammar.json): its SHA256 is
# pinned as a literal in tests/test_grow0_grammar_file.py and in the GROW-0 PREREG
# §2, both computed from the LF-blob bytes. Same class of bug as the pins above —
# a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrote this file's on-disk bytes to
# CRLF, so load_grammar_with_hash_check() (lab/discovery/grammar.py) correctly, but
# spuriously, reported GrammarDriftError against a checkout artifact, not a real
# edit to the frozen grammar (found running the GROW-0 harness's full-scale
# invocation 2026-08-22, zero content difference confirmed via git show HEAD).
discovery_manifests/grow0_grammar.json text eol=lf

# Append-only logs (reverse-chron / additions-only). Every branch inserts a new
# entry at the SAME top anchor, so the default merge conflicts on parallel
# worktrees (e.g. the 2026-06-07 3-way SESSIONS.md collision across PRs
# #153/#154/#155 + #156). The built-in `union` driver keeps BOTH sides' added
# lines (no conflict markers) on merge AND rebase. Caveats: union does not ORDER
# the merged entries (an occasional manual reorder / doubled `---` tidy), and it
# won't flag two edits to the SAME existing line — acceptable for add-at-top logs.
#
# The ordering caveat is no longer carried by hand for docs/SESSIONS.md (it still
# is for the other two). `roll_sessions.py --check-order` gates it at pre-commit
# and the post-merge hook repairs it, ordering same-date entries by the author
# time of the commit that first added each heading. Repair is scoped to the top
# window — the only region union can disturb — so hand-placed historical entries
# are never re-sorted; mark a deliberate same-day order in the heading itself
# (the `2026-07-29 (later)` convention).
docs/SESSIONS.md merge=union
docs/rejected_candidates.md merge=union
# RETAINED 2026-08-08 (reviewed, not inherited): 6 lifetime commits, never rolled, no recorded
# union-merge incident. The resurrection hazard needs a roll to fire, so this stays as-is rather
# than being removed. Note the asymmetry: only docs/SESSIONS.md has an ordering gate
# (gates.yml `sessions-order`); rejected_candidates.md and this file have none, so contradictory
# entries can merge cleanly and silently. Same retain-as-inert precedent as the fixtures pin above.
docs/mc_anchor_history.md merge=union

# Container-build files whose line endings are load-bearing. A Windows clone with
# core.autocrlf=true would check these out as CRLF: the .dockerignore's patterns
# would carry a trailing \r and silently fail to match (breaking the default-exclude
# allow-list → files missing from the Fly build context), and the Dockerfile is
# cleaner as LF. Pinned to LF so any clone builds the c1 rail image correctly.
.dockerignore text eol=lf
deploy/c1_rail/Dockerfile text eol=lf
